                                                                          <section class="ai-alert__tldr">
          <h2 class="ai-alert__tldr-title">TL;DR</h2>
          <ul class="ai-alert__tldr-list">
                          <li>TTR-Compressor's convolutional variant beats the strongest attention-based baseline on RULER retrieval tasks by 12.24 points.</li>                          <li>As a visual-token merger it matches the standard MLP merger while using 55.6% fewer visual tokens.</li>                          <li>Under 3×3 merging the convolutional variant improves on the MLP merger by 4.10 on OCRBench, 4.54 on InfoVQA and 3.56 on ChartQA.</li>                      </ul>
        </section>
      
      
      <div class="ai-alert__essay">
        <p>TTR-Compressor, a sequence compression operator from researchers at the Chinese University of Hong Kong, Token Foundry and Alibaba Group, matches the widely used MLP visual-token merger while using 55.6% fewer visual tokens, and in its convolutional variant beats the strongest attention-based compressor on RULER retrieval tasks by 12.24 points.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.31574" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">paper on Hugging Face</a> reframes sequence compression — the step that collapses a block of tokens into one summary vector — as a test-time regression problem. Input tokens define a ridge-regression system; its closed-form coefficients become the compressed representation; the regression itself is learned end-to-end by backpropagation. The authors argue existing operators share &quot;a fundamental structural limitation&quot;: they &quot;aggregate tokens using weights that do not account for inter-token relationships.&quot; Mean pooling uses uniform weights, projection uses fixed position-dependent weights, attention-weighted pooling scores tokens independently. The consequence, the paper writes, is that &quot;redundant tokens can reinforce one another, while distinctive information is diluted.&quot;</p>
<p>For visual-token merging the authors swap the standard MLP merger inside a Qwen3-VL ViT + Qwen3-4B-Instruct stack. Under 2×2 merging the average score rises from 54.93 to 55.92, and the convolutional variant reaches 56.03. Under more aggressive 3×3 merging the gains concentrate on text-rich benchmarks: +4.10 on OCRBench, +4.54 on InfoVQA, +3.56 on ChartQA, for +2.58 on average. The 3×3 convolutional variant averages 54.85, close to the MLP merger&#039;s 54.93 at 2×2 while using fewer than half the visual tokens.</p>
<p>The compressor adds only 2d trainable parameters per block via a T×T Cholesky solve. At a larger 1B-parameter / 100B-token setting the authors report 0.7996 average RULER accuracy against 0.735 for attention-weighted pooling, with the gap widening at longer context lengths. It is one of 98 <a href="https://aiweekly.co/ai-news-today/multimodal-ai-news">multimodal stories</a> we have tracked in the last 90 days.</p>
      </div>

              <aside class="ai-alert__editor-note">
          <span class="ai-alert__editor-note-label">Editor's note</span>
          <div class="ai-alert__editor-note-body">The 55.6% visual-token reduction is a direct lever on MLLM serving cost if it survives at frontier scale; with Alibaba listed on the paper, the obvious next question is whether this quietly lands in a Qwen-VL variant before competing stacks catch on.</div>
        </aside>
      
                
